---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { siteConfig } from '../config/site';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { name: 'Privacy Explained', href: '/privacy-explained/' },
];

const articleJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'How Zero-Upload Privacy Works on ZixTool',
  description: 'Technical explanation and step-by-step verification guide for client-side, in-browser file conversion without server uploads.',
  url: `${siteConfig.url}/privacy-explained/`,
  author: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: siteConfig.brand,
  },
  datePublished: '2026-11-13',
  dateModified: '2026-11-16',
};
---

<Layout
  title={`In-Browser Privacy Explained & Verified | ${siteConfig.brand}`}
  description={`Zero-upload privacy explained: learn how ${siteConfig.brand} processes photos in browser memory. Verify in your Network tab that files never upload to servers.`}
  canonical={`${siteConfig.url}/privacy-explained/`}
  jsonLd={articleJsonLd}
>
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-8">
    <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs} />

    <article>
      <header class="mb-10">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800 border border-emerald-200/80 mb-3">
          Technical Architecture & Proof
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight mb-4">
          Zero-Upload In-Browser Privacy Explained
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
          Welcome to our client-side privacy explained guide with step-by-step verification instructions. Learn why your photos and documents never leave your physical device, how our client-side engine executes in browser memory, and how you can independently verify it in 15 seconds.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none space-y-8 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-base sm:text-lg">
        <section class="bg-emerald-50/70 border border-emerald-200/80 rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 shadow-card not-prose">
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-emerald-950 mb-3">The Short Answer</h2>
          <p class="text-emerald-900 leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base">
            With our zero-upload privacy explained, you can see why your data stays safe. When you convert an image, reduce file size, or perform calculations on {siteConfig.brand}, <strong>zero file bytes are transmitted over the internet</strong>. All decompression, pixel rendering, format encoding, and mathematical calculations are executed by your device's CPU and GPU via standard JavaScript, Web Workers, and the HTML5 Canvas API.
          </p>
        </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">How Server-Based Converters Work (The Risk)</h2>
        <p>
          Traditional online file converters—such as CloudConvert, FreeConvert, and TinyPNG—operate using backend server farms. When you drag a file into their drop zone:
        </p>
        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li>Your private photo or document is uploaded across the public internet to remote cloud instances.</li>
          <li>The file sits in remote server temporary storage queues (often retained for 1 to 48 hours according to their privacy policies).</li>
          <li>A server-side command-line tool (such as ImageMagick or FFmpeg) processes the file.</li>
          <li>You download the converted asset back across the internet.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          This model exposes personal family pictures, ID scans, company contracts, and medical documents to server breaches, logging, third-party cloud hosting retention, and ISP eavesdropping.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">How {siteConfig.brand} Eliminates Server Uploads</h2>
        <p>
          Modern web browsers are full-featured operating environments capable of high-performance multimedia computing. {siteConfig.brand} eliminates server intermediaries completely:
        </p>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 not-prose my-6">
          <div class="p-5 bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl shadow-xs">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">1. Local Decoding</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
              Files selected via file input or drag-and-drop are loaded as in-memory <code>Blob</code> and <code>ArrayBuffer</code> objects directly within your browser process.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl shadow-xs">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">2. In-Memory Canvas</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
              Pixels are drawn into an off-screen HTML5 Canvas. Image re-encoding (e.g. to JPG or WebP) is performed client-side by browser native codecs.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl shadow-xs">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">3. Blob URL Download</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
              Output files are served via <code>blob:</code> URLs referencing local RAM. Saving the file writes straight from browser memory to your disk.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <figure class="my-6 p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white border border-slate-200 shadow-2xs text-center not-prose">
          <img
            src="/images/tools/pdf-security-infographic.svg"
            alt="Client-Side In-Browser Privacy Explained Architecture Diagram"
            width="720"
            height="360"
            class="max-w-full h-auto mx-auto rounded-xl"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption class="mt-2.5 text-xs text-slate-500 font-medium">
            <strong>Architecture Diagram:</strong> Client-side RAM sandboxing with zero network byte transmissions.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Step-by-Step: Verify It in Your Own Browser</h2>
        <p>
          You do not need to take our word for it. You can independently confirm that zero bytes are transmitted by inspecting your browser's built-in developer network monitor:
        </p>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-2xl p-6 not-prose space-y-4 text-sm font-mono shadow-card">
          <div class="flex items-center space-x-2 text-emerald-400 font-bold border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
            <span>&gt; Verification Protocol (15-Second Test)</span>
          </div>
          <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300">
            <li>Open any converter on {siteConfig.domain} (e.g., <a href="/heic-to-jpg/" class="text-emerald-400 underline hover:text-emerald-300">HEIC to JPG</a>).</li>
            <li>Press <kbd class="px-1.5 py-0.5 bg-slate-800 rounded text-slate-200">F12</kbd> (or right-click anywhere and select <strong>Inspect</strong>).</li>
            <li>Click on the <strong>Network</strong> tab in Developer Tools.</li>
            <li>Check the <strong>Fetch/XHR</strong> filter to see all background HTTP requests.</li>
            <li>Drop a 5MB or 10MB photo into the tool and click <strong>Convert</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>Observe the Network tab:</strong> No POST requests, no upload streams, and zero outgoing image bytes.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Offline & Airplane Mode Compatibility</h2>
        <p>
          Because our tools require no backend compute server, you can load the tool page while connected to Wi-Fi, turn on <strong>Airplane Mode</strong> (or unplug your Ethernet cable), and convert images or run calculations seamlessly offline.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>

    <div class="mt-12 pt-6 border-t border-slate-200 flex items-center justify-between text-xs text-slate-500">
      <span>Last reviewed: <time datetime="2026-11-16">16 Nov 2026</time></span>
      <span>Published by: {siteConfig.brand} Engineering Team</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

